  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 AM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nEarly morning RAP analysis shows broad northwesterly flow aloft as \nsprawling ridging over the southern half of the CONUS is only \npartially depressed by a closed low over Hudson Bay. Minor ripples \nin the flow reflect mesoscale disturbances embedded within the \nnorthwest flow, and while the most impactful disturbances will \nremain south of the UP, some small troughs in the vicinity will \nerode the presence of ridging aloft/surface high pressure that would \notherwise have too much subsidence for any precipitation to occur. \nCAMs show little agreement in the details of showers and potential \nthunder today (which is to be expected in weakly forced, nebulous \npatterns such as this), but lake breezes may serve as a focal point \nfor showers and a few thunderstorms today. With weak, unidirectional \nflow, the shear pattern will not be supportive of severe weather, \nthough the HREF SBCAPE solutions offer a wide spread as to how \nbuoyant updrafts may become. While the ensemble minimum for SBCAPE \nis genuinely 0, the ensemble mean is around 500 J/kg with ensemble \nmaximums in the 1000+ J/kg territory, so some thunder remains \npossible (maybe 1 or two strong thunderstorms cannot be ruled out). \nAs to what probability exists for those storms to occur, that will \nbe a moving target through the day. For now, HREF chances of a \nshower capable of producing a tenth of an inch of rain within 25km \nof any point in the interior UP is between 30-60% with chances of a \nthunderstorm capable of producing modeled radar reflectivity \nexceeding 40 dBZ is around 15%.\n\nClarity on the weather pattern is not really provided on Wednesday \neither as the 500mb pattern remains much the same as today, so a \ngeneral repeat of today is expected. All eyes turn upstream to a \ntrough that will be over the Seattle/Vancouver area by Wednesday \n18Z. This trough will provide the best mechanism to progress the \nweather pattern, though it will first kick ridging from over the \nRockies and Plains to be over the Midwest and Great Lakes late this \nweek. This will lead to rising surface pressure and dry conditions \nfor virtually all of the UP Thursday and Friday.\n\nThe aforementioned kicking trough arrives over the UP Saturday. \nUnfortunately, spread in the track and depth of this trough passage \nis high as at the same time as the trough arrives, ridging builds \nover the Southeast, allowing for ensembles to have a spread of \nsolutions regarding how this trough fares against either a weaker or \nstronger Southeast ridge. Current LREF chances of a wetting rain \n(around a tenth of an inch/24hr) is around 45% by Sunday afternoon. \nThunderstorms are uncertain as well, though the LREF mean SBCAPE by \n00Z Sunday being around 15 J/kg is not an encouraging sign for those \nlooking forward to a rumble of thunder. While there is \nunderstandable spread on the pattern that follows, it is generally \nexpected that by Sunday evening and through Monday night, ridging \nresumes in the wake of the trough, with next Tuesday looking to \nresume the ongoing pattern of weak precip signals in persistent \nNWerly flow.",
